  "phases": [
    {
      "app": "camera",
      "message": "I filmed Dad's whole pile of mail on the kitchen table and took a few photos. Go through every sheet and tell me what each one is and what it asks of us. Anything you couldn't read, ask me.",
      "nudges": 2
    },
    {
      "app": "plan",
      "message": "This is his insurance portal. Match the bills from the paper to the EOBs and work out what he actually owes each provider, and anything that looks wrong.",
      "nudges": 2
    },
    {
      "app": "riverside",
      "message": "Now the hospital's guest-pay page; it wants the statement numbers from the paper. Dispute what's wrong and pay what he genuinely owes.",
      "nudges": 2
    },
    {
      "app": "cedar",
      "message": "Cedar is where the anesthesia group, the imaging center, the dermatologist and the home health agency bill. Same thing here.",
      "nudges": 1
    },
    {
      "app": "plan",
      "message": "Back in the insurance portal. Appeal any denial that's worth appealing and still in time, with whatever the paper proves.",
      "nudges": 1
    }
  ],
  "tables": {
    "plan": [
      {
        "topic": "deductible",
        "text": "In-network deductible $1,500 per plan year (January 1 to December 31). 2026: met on 2026-04-02. After the deductible the plan pays 75% of the allowed amount; the member pays 25%."
      },
      {
        "topic": "network",
        "text": "In network: Riverside General Hospital, Riverside Physicians Group (Riverside's physician billing), Northgate Imaging, Lakeshore Dermatology, Brightwater Home Health. Out of network: Keystone Anesthesia Partners."
      },
      {
        "topic": "surprise_billing",
        "text": "No Surprises Act: an out-of-network clinician who treats you at an in-network facility may not bill you more than your in-network cost sharing, the patient responsibility on the EOB. A balance bill above it can be disputed with the clinician."
      },
      {
        "topic": "eob",
        "text": "On a Final EOB, patient responsibility is the most an in-network provider may bill for that claim. A claim In process is not decided: do not pay or dispute its bill yet. A Reprocessed EOB replaces the earlier one; if you paid more than the new patient responsibility, ask the provider for a refund."
      },
      {
        "topic": "appeals",
        "text": "Internal appeal: file within 180 days of the date of the denial notice, with the reason and supporting records (a prior authorization number, a clinician's letter). One internal appeal per denial."
      },
      {
        "topic": "prior_auth",
        "text": "Advanced imaging (MRI, CT) needs prior authorization. An approval letter carries its authorization number; a claim denied for no authorization while one was approved is a plan error the member may appeal with that number."
      }
    ],
